Subject: [PATCH net-next 0/1] udp: one wakeup per drained receive batch

Since commit b650bf0977d3 ("udp: remove busylock and add per NUMA
queues"), a UDP receive can move several skbs to sk_receive_queue at
once when several CPUs deliver to the same socket, and it then calls
sk_data_ready() once per skb. With a poll/epoll waiter on the socket,
that runs the waitqueue walk and ep_poll_callback() nb times for one
change of state. This patch wakes the socket once with
nr_exclusive = nb, so blocking readers still get up to nb wakeups but
poll/epoll entries are called once.

Measured on the patched kernel in an x86_64 guest (2 boots, 9 base
boots): ep_poll_callback() calls per datagram go from 1.00 to 0.87-0.91
with 6 senders and to 0.45-0.47 with 12; unchanged with 1 sender. At
the same offered load the patched kernel drops no more datagrams than
the base kernel, and blocking readers lose no datagram and get no late
wakeup. The question for reviewers is whether this is worth a new
wakeup helper.

  # | summary                          | impact             | evidence
  --+----------------------------------+--------------------+-----------
  1 | udp: one sk_data_ready call per  | medium: a UDP      | measured
    | drained batch (adds              | socket fed by      | (call
    | __wake_up_sync_key_nr())         | several CPUs       | counts)

Two other patches from the same work are not in this posting:
- "eventpoll: keep the fields ep_poll_callback() writes in one
  cacheline" was dropped: no measurable difference in cycles or L1
  misses per callback.
- "ipv4: send IP ID 0 for connected non-TCP atomic datagrams" was
  dropped: it changes the IP ID on the wire for an unmeasured saving,
  and restarting the ID at 0 on every UDP GSO send would stop receive
  GRO merging across sends.

Testing:
- Builds with W=1 (net/core/sock.o, net/ipv4/udp.o, net/ipv6/udp.o,
  kernel/sched/build_utility.o, net/core/skmsg.o, net/sunrpc/xprtsock.o),
  x86_64. sock_def_readable() disassembles to the same code as before
  apart from one register load (nr_exclusive = 1).
- On the unpatched host kernel (6.18): bpftrace count of
  sock_def_readable() calls per __udp_enqueue_schedule_skb() call with
  1-24 senders into one socket (table in the patch), and a check that
  4 threads blocked in recv() on one socket lose no datagrams and see no
  late wakeup. These validate the test program only.
- On the patched kernel (x86_64 guest, nested KVM, 16 vCPUs): the
  ep_poll_callback() counts above (2 boots); the blocking-reader check
  (2 boots, 4 runs each); a fixed-rate test, 12 senders at 50k-200k
  datagrams/s, 3 boots of each kernel interleaved, comparing drops and
  receiver CPU time per datagram. No kernel warnings.

Not tested:
- Hardware other than a nested KVM guest; wall-clock throughput;
  sockmap/sunrpc UDP sockets (code path unchanged); O_ASYNC; IPv6.
- Not rebased on or tested against net-next; base is v7.3-rc3
  (518e5b794c06).
- No selftest added.

Tool assistance: the changes, changelogs and measurements in this
series were produced with Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) as a
coding assistant, prompted to find and fix performance problems in the
client-side networking path and to measure them. The patch carries an
Assisted-by tag.

